{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_bdrk_014xidHF4VXSVhfrDzMXw2BN","type":"tool_result","content":"=== cvt.rs (hand-restored region) ===\n#[inline]\npub fn convert(value: f64) -> c_int {\n    // NaN never compares in-range, but check explicitly for clarity.\n    if value.is_nan() {\n        return c_int::MIN;\n    }\n\n    // `cvttsd2si` truncates toward zero, then reports the indefinite value if\n    // the truncated result is outside the destination range. Both bounds are\n    // exactly representable as `f64`, so these comparisons are exact.\n    let truncated = value.trunc();\n    if truncated >= -2_147_483_648.0_f64 && truncated <= 2_147_483_647.0_f64 {\n        truncated as c_int\n    } else {\n        c_int::MIN\n    }\n}\n\n=== buffer.rs (hand-restored region) ===\n    // same contract the C function relies on.\n    let out = unsafe { core::slice::from_raw_parts_mut(buffer, size as usize) };\n\n    for (i, slot) in out.iter_mut().enumerate() {\n        let value = seed.wrapping_add((i as c_int).wrapping_mul(7)) % 256;\n        // Rust's `%` truncates toward zero just like C's, and `as c_char`\n        // performs the same truncating conversion GCC applies for `(char)`.\n        *slot = value as c_char;\n    }\n}\n\n=== final clean run of everything ===\n  symbols    test result: ok. 3 passed; 0 failed\n  configs    test result: ok. 26 passed; 0 failed\n  errors     test result: ok. 28 passed; 0 failed\n  doubleneg  test result: ok. 9 passed; 0 failed","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"7916fcfe-1ecc-4e84-9174-9e7480706eb2","uuid":"fe77f796-3613-4c1b-a03d-3a2bcfbc11c0","timestamp":"2026-08-28T00:42:01.139Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"=== cvt.rs (hand-restored region) ===\n#[inline]\npub fn convert(value: f64) -> c_int {\n    // NaN never compares in-range, but check explicitly for clarity.\n    if value.is_nan() {\n        return c_int::MIN;\n    }\n\n    // `cvttsd2si` truncates toward zero, then reports the indefinite value if\n    // the truncated result is outside the destination range. Both bounds are\n    // exactly representable as `f64`, so these comparisons are exact.\n    let truncated = value.trunc();\n    if truncated >= -2_147_483_648.0_f64 && truncated <= 2_147_483_647.0_f64 {\n        truncated as c_int\n    } else {\n        c_int::MIN\n    }\n}\n\n=== buffer.rs (hand-restored region) ===\n    // same contract the C function relies on.\n    let out = unsafe { core::slice::from_raw_parts_mut(buffer, size as usize) };\n\n    for (i, slot) in out.iter_mut().enumerate() {\n        let value = seed.wrapping_add((i as c_int).wrapping_mul(7)) % 256;\n        // Rust's `%` truncates toward zero just like C's, and `as c_char`\n        // performs the same truncating conversion GCC applies for `(char)`.\n        *slot = value as c_char;\n    }\n}\n\n=== final clean run of everything ===\n  symbols    test result: ok. 3 passed; 0 failed\n  configs    test result: ok. 26 passed; 0 failed\n  errors     test result: ok. 28 passed; 0 failed\n  doubleneg  test result: ok. 9 passed; 0 failed","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_bdrk_qodso5bdt22mzflyemysi5ekn7c3ndrouf3c4h43g7ykfaphmfdq","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Verification complete. All four phases passed, and the source is back to its pristine state.\n\n## Outcome\n\n**The Rust translation is byte-for-byte correct. No translation defects were found — I made zero changes to `translation/src/`.** The two bugs I did find and fix were in my own test infrastructure.\n\n### Phase A — artifacts (in `translation/`)\n| File | Content |\n|---|---|\n| `SYMBOLS.md` | All 6 C exports, all present in Rust. Symbol diff `C \\ Rust` = **empty**. 0 unresolved non-libc symbols. |\n| `ERRORS.md` | **28 rows** derived by grepping every rejection in `lib.c` (there are no error enums/asserts — every rejection is a sentinel, a guarded branch, or a `cvttsd2si` out-of-range). |\n| `CONFIGS.md` | **34 rows** over 12 branch axes (double classes, `memchr` hit position × size, needle truncation, seed sign/overflow, `b==0`, `c%10`, truthiness patterns). |\n\n### Phases B–D — results\n- **66 tests**, all passing across **4 configurations** (release/debug × default/`--no-default-features`; the crate declares no `[features]`, so that's the complete cross-product). Every row 1..28 and 1..34 maps to a test function — verified programmatically, 0 unchecked boxes, 0 orphan tests.\n- All calls go through `dlsym` on both `.so` files; `doubleneg` is compared on **both** its return value **and** its full ~40-line `printf` byte stream (via fd-1 redirection).\n\n### Two infrastructure bugs I found and fixed\n1. **`cargo test` never rebuilds a `cdylib`.** With `crate-type = [\"cdylib\"]`, `cargo test` compiles `src/lib.rs` into a *test-harness binary* and leaves the `.so` untouched — so the suite was silently validating a 12-minute-old library. I caught this because a deliberately broken `convert_double_to_int` still \"passed\" everything. Fixed with `run_all_features.sh` (builds before testing) plus a `assert_libraries_are_fresh` guard that refuses to run against a stale `.so`.\n2. **libtest's parallel progress output leaked into the fd-1 captures**, corrupting `doubleneg` comparisons. Fixed by moving both capturing suites to `harness = false` with a sequential runner that reports on stderr.\n\n### Suite integrity check (`mutation_check.py`)\nRather than trust green tests, I injected 15 one-line bugs one at a time: **13 caught, 2 provably semantically equivalent, 0 unexplained survivors.** The equivalences are proven, not assumed — e.g. `%` vs `rem_euclid` is undetectable here because they differ by exactly 256 and the following `as i8` keeps only the low byte (confirmed exhaustively in C: 0 differences over 200,001 values).\n\n### Two notable findings about the C code\n- **`cvttsd2si` premise confirmed by disassembly**, not assumed: CMake sets no `CMAKE_BUILD_TYPE`, so the C builds at `-O0` and GCC emits a real `cvttsd2si` rather than constant-folding the `INFINITY`/`NAN` casts. The Rust correctly reproduces the `INT_MIN` \"integer indefinite\" result instead of Rust's saturating `as i32`.\n- **Two `else` branches in `doubleneg` are dead code.** Since `gcd(7, 256) = 1`, `create_numeric_buffer(buf, 256, seed)` always writes a *permutation of all 256 byte values*, so `\"Value %d not found\"` and the `direct_search == NULL` skip can never execute for any input. I rewrote those `ERRORS.md` rows to state this with the proof and cover the underlying rejection where it *is* reachable (`find_value_in_buffer`), rather than claiming coverage I couldn't have.\n\nOne test expectation of mine was genuinely wrong and I fixed the test, not the code: `2147483647.9999999` isn't representable as `f64` and rounds *up* to `2^31`, making it out-of-range."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3013,"cache_read_input_tokens":159962,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":3013,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"7916fcfe-1ecc-4e84-9174-9e7480706eb2","uuid":"c9c90e84-fdb7-432e-b04c-dd1c4843713d","timestamp":"2026-08-28T00:42:26.005Z"}
